ESP-IDF v6.1 nvs_flash, patch 0003 (upstream issue espressif/esp-idf#19214, IDFGH-18475)

Problem: when loading the active page, NVS checks whether the first entry the state table
calls empty was half-written by a power cut by reading only its first 4-byte word. A cut write
that left the first word erased and later words programmed is taken as free; the next write
lands on the leftover bits, returns ESP_OK, is then found with a CRC mismatch and erased - the
key and (for an update) its previous value are lost. (Reproduced in firmware/test/nvs_linux
with the "first word erased" flash model; whether a chip can tear a program that way is not
specified by the datasheets checked upstream.)

Fix: check every word of the entry. Reads 32 bytes instead of 4 once per page load. The
on-flash format and every write are unchanged.

--- a/src/nvs_page.cpp
+++ b/src/nvs_page.cpp
@@ -693,13 +693,16 @@
             if (err != ESP_OK) {
                 return err;
             }
-            uint32_t header;
-            auto rc = mPartition->read_raw(entryAddress, &header, sizeof(header));
+            // a cut write can leave the first word erased and later ones
+            // programmed, so every word of the entry is checked
+            const size_t WORD_COUNT = ENTRY_SIZE / sizeof(uint32_t);
+            uint32_t words[WORD_COUNT];
+            auto rc = mPartition->read_raw(entryAddress, words, sizeof(words));
             if (rc != ESP_OK) {
                 mState = PageState::INVALID;
                 return rc;
             }
-            if (header != 0xffffffff) {
+            if (std::any_of(words, words + WORD_COUNT, [](uint32_t val) -> bool { return val != 0xffffffff; })) {
                 auto oldState = state;
                 rc = mEntryTable.get(mNextFreeEntry, &oldState);
                 if (rc != ESP_OK) {
